{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1784023074,"nonce":"xB2vZXNfkN4J6R9yLfgCKLbm7RE=","title":"Give an invoked pipeline an **explicit, compile-validated interface** (ADR-0038): the lib declares the `inputs:` (params the caller must supply) and `outputs:` (results it exposes); the caller passes inputs on the invoke step. Explicit over ambient — the same stance ADR-0037 took for secret scope. Reuse the existing param/result convention (ADR-0008: `SCARAB_PARAM_*` in, results out) as the runtime mechanism.","message":"Note: the authored **syntax** for declaring/passing params (e.g. `with:` on the invoke step, `inputs:` header on the lib) is the one open design detail in this slice — pick the smallest thing consistent with existing params and record it in the commit.\n\n\n- [ ] A lib pipeline can declare required `inputs:` and exposed `outputs:`.\n- [ ] The caller supplies inputs on the invoke step; they reach the inlined steps via the existing param convention.\n- [ ] Missing required inputs or unknown/extra inputs are rejected at compile with diagnostics.\n- [ ] A referenced output that the lib does not expose is a compile error.\n- [ ] Tests: satisfied interface, missing input, extra input, undeclared output.\n\n\n- a8d536a ([invoke] Step kind + single-level compile-time inlining)","files":null}]}